WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:05.720
2017's It has been scaring audiences everywhere
since its release, but for all the terror

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:09.880
Pennywise is causing on the big screen, the
novel contains even more horrifying scenes

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:11.480
that had to be cut from the film.

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:16.279
Here are the scariest moments from It that
were too terrifying to leave the book.

00:00:16.279 --> 00:00:17.900
Ben's first encounter

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:21.919
Early in the novel, Ben Hanscom is shown volunteering
to stay late to help his teacher finish some

00:00:21.919 --> 00:00:25.589
last-minute tasks, and by the time they're
done, the streets have cleared.

00:00:25.589 --> 00:00:27.481
Even though it's brutally cold, Ben decides
to walk home alone.

00:00:27.481 --> 00:00:32.360
Along the way, he spots a clown standing on
the ice, clutching balloons and offering Ben

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:34.940
one, with his usual offer for the kid to float.

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:38.989
What's frightening about it is that Ben does
want that balloon and feels sucked into Pennywise's

00:00:38.989 --> 00:00:43.329
mental grip, which is only broken by the town's
clock chiming to indicate the top of the hour.

00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:45.300
Talk about being saved by the bell.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.059
The werewolf

00:00:51.059 --> 00:00:54.989
The It movie did a great job of sneaking in
a lot of the pop culture-inspired monsters

00:00:54.989 --> 00:00:58.670
that loomed large over the Losers, but the
werewolf at the Neibolt Street house only

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:00.949
appeared in a blink-and-you'd-miss-it moment.

00:01:00.949 --> 00:01:04.250
As in the movie, the werewolf arrives during
Bill and Richie's journey into the house.

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:08.600
But while Richie's perspective shows It as
the four-legged freak show, Bill sees something

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:09.600
different.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.790
As they escape on Bill's bike Silver, he describes
It as a clown in a varsity coat, which runs

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:15.930
counter to Richie's memory of the monster.

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:19.590
It's a subtle difference, but the scene proves
that It looks different to different people

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:23.409
depending on their particular fear, which
makes it that much scarier than some bozo

00:01:23.409 --> 00:01:24.520
with big teeth.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:28.229
The absence of the wolf also means that the
book's weapon — silver slugs — aren't

00:01:28.229 --> 00:01:31.930
a factor in the movie, which is why the Losers
end up fighting with a bolt gun instead of

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:34.049
their trusty slingshot.

00:01:34.049 --> 00:01:35.500
The standpipe

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:39.670
Although Stan's first encounter with It in
the movie takes place in his father's office,

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:41.780
the literary version is a lot creepier.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:45.910
As a child, Stan is an avid bird watcher and
heads down to the Barrens to do some spotting

00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:50.240
near the Standpipe — a notoriously off-limits
part of town, since it claimed too many lives

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:51.810
when it was open to the public.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:55.100
After seeing the Standpipe appear to float,
Stanley goes inside.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:59.320
The door shuts, locking him in, and he hears
a voice claiming to be "the dead ones."

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:03.200
He only manages to escape by chanting the
names of birds from his guidebook.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.120
Despite what he's seen, Stan still has trouble
wrapping his head around what's happened and

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:09.140
needs more proof of It's existence.

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:13.580
This all goes to show, of course, that Stan
has more trouble accepting It than the rest,

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:17.120
which will have deadly consequences for him
later in life.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:18.500
Georgie's picture

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:22.800
In the It movie, the kids' projector slideshow
develops a mind of Its own and slowly churns

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.270
out a more-than-photorealistic image of Pennywise.

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:28.500
That might be a clever way of getting the
feel of the scene across while embracing the

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:32.230
movie's adjusted timeline, but there's still
something particularly sinister about the

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:34.060
way this moment goes down in the book.

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:38.210
As reimagined by the TV miniseries as well,
Bill looks at a photo album with some innocent

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:39.540
family pictures of Georgie.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:43.650
But one photo of his lost little brother starts
to move, winking at him and promising that

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:46.830
he'll see him soon — just before the book
starts gushing blood.

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:50.701
Making matters worse is that the image represents
Bill's freshest memory of Georgie, since it

00:02:50.701 --> 00:02:52.610
was taken just days before his death.

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:56.500
Sure, the It movie brought Georgie back to
terrorize Bill, but the scene as written is

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:59.030
a spine-tingler all on its own.

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:00.660
The disappearance of Chad Lowe

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:05.370
Other than Betty Ripsom and Patrick Hockstetter,
the children who've gone missing in the 2017

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:09.340
movie are largely ignored, which means the
movie also passes over one of the most disturbing

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:11.600
disappearances of all: Chad Lowe.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:15.230
Grown-up Mike Hanlon makes mention of the
boy later when recapping the town's attitude

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:19.410
towards the missing kids, pointing out Chad's
case to the cop who wants to wave away the

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:21.930
deaths or disappearances as coincidence.

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:25.020
Lowe was just three and a half when he went
missing the same year the Losers made their

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:26.630
first stand against It.

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:30.950
Granted, Pennywise has never seemed to care
about the ages of his victims; after all,

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:33.240
Georgie's just six when he's snatched away
on the street.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:37.530
But little Chad is still just a toddler, which
makes his probable death even more gruesome.

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:41.170
It's a footnote to the whole story of It,
but an especially unsettling one.

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:42.210
Thanks for watching!

